# Copy this file to deploy/.deploy.env and fill in your server.
#
#   cp deploy/deploy.env.example deploy/.deploy.env
#
# .deploy.env is gitignored. It holds where your server is, not any secret —
# the PIN pepper and the bot token live on the server, in /opt/sank/.env, and
# never travel with the code.
#
# There are TWO env files in this project and they have nothing to do with each
# other. This one is read by deploy.sh, on your Mac, and the app never sees it.
# The server's own settings are the second half of this file.

# Where to ssh. The user is `sank`, not root: setup-server.sh copied your ssh
# key over to it and gave it permission to restart the service, and nothing
# about a deploy needs root. An entry from ~/.ssh/config works here too, e.g.
# SSH_HOST=sank-vps
SSH_HOST=sank@203.0.113.10

# ssh port, if your provider moved it.
SSH_PORT=22

# Where the app lives on the server. Matches deploy/setup-server.sh.
REMOTE_DIR=/opt/sank

# The health gate: what deploy.sh curls after restarting the service, FROM the
# server itself. 127.0.0.1 is right — the app binds to localhost and the
# firewall keeps port 3100 closed, so this is the only address that answers.
HEALTH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3100/api/health

# How patient the health gate is: 15 tries, 2 seconds apart, each waiting at
# most 5 seconds for an answer = up to ~30 seconds before it rolls back. The app
# opens the database, runs the migrations and re-applies every trigger at
# startup, so give it room on a big database.
HEALTH_RETRIES=15
HEALTH_INTERVAL=2
HEALTH_TIMEOUT=5

# Old releases kept on the server, in case you need to look at one. Each is
# roughly the size of .output (tens of MB).
KEEP_RELEASES=5


# ============================================================================
# --- and, separately: /opt/sank/.env on the server ---
# ============================================================================
#
# NOT this file. A different file, on a different machine, read by a different
# program: systemd reads it when the service starts and hands it to the app.
# Every change there needs `sudo systemctl restart sank`.
#
# setup-server.sh writes a starter version of it, with a freshly generated
# PIN_PEPPER, and never touches it again. This block is the reference for what
# belongs in it — the whole list, so nothing has to be guessed.
#
# These names are plain. There is no prefix: the server reads every one of them
# straight from `process.env`, so what you write is what the code sees.
#
# Copy-pasteable, in full:
#
#   DB_PATH=/opt/sank/data/sank.db
#   PIN_PEPPER=<64 hex characters — see below>
#   TRUST_PROXY=1
#   PUBLIC_URL=https://sank.example.ba
#   NITRO_PORT=3100
#   NITRO_HOST=127.0.0.1
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_PATH=/opt/sank/data/sank.db
#   The database file. Absolute, and outside the release directories on purpose:
#   the releases come and go, the data does not. The systemd unit sets this too;
#   the line here is what makes it visible to a human reading one file.
#
# PIN_PEPPER=
#   32 random bytes, as 64 hex characters. Mixed into every PIN and password
#   before it is hashed, so a stolen database file cannot be attacked with a
#   pre-computed table of all ten thousand four-digit PINs. Generate one with:
#
#       openssl rand -hex 32
#
#   CHANGING THIS INVALIDATES EVERY STORED PIN. Not "logs everybody out" —
#   invalidates: no PIN in the database verifies against the new pepper, and
#   nobody can log in until the owner sets all of them again in /a. Generate it
#   once, keep it with the backups (a password manager), and leave it alone.
#
#   An EMPTY pepper is also a working configuration — the code falls back to no
#   pepper and says nothing — which is the trap. deploy.sh warns when it finds
#   this line empty, because filling it in later has the same effect as changing
#   it.
#
# TRUST_PROXY=1
#   Believe the X-Real-IP / X-Forwarded-For headers when deciding who is
#   calling. Required behind nginx: the app would otherwise see 127.0.0.1 for
#   every request on earth, and every rate-limit bucket and PIN lockout would be
#   shared by the whole internet.
#
#   ONLY SAFE BECAUSE deploy/nginx.conf EXISTS AND IS ENABLED. That file
#   overwrites both headers with the real socket address; without it, a caller
#   sends his own header and picks his own bucket. If nginx is ever removed,
#   remove this line with it.
#
# COOKIE_SECURE
#   Not in the list above, on purpose. It DEFAULTS TO TRUE, and the only value
#   that does anything is `COOKIE_SECURE=0`, which turns it off. A secure cookie
#   is only ever sent over https, so 0 belongs in a dev laptop's .env and NEVER
#   on the VPS. Leaving it out entirely is the correct production setting — the
#   default is deliberately the safe one, so that a forgotten line is merely
#   inconvenient in dev instead of silently insecure in production.
#
# PUBLIC_URL=https://sank.example.ba
#   Where the app answers as the outside world sees it — the address the owner
#   types on his phone. Same host as `server_name` in nginx.conf.
#
# NITRO_PORT=3100
# NITRO_HOST=127.0.0.1
#   The port and the interface the app listens on. 127.0.0.1 means "this machine
#   only": the firewall never opens 3100 and nginx is what publishes the app.
#   The systemd unit sets both as well; do not change one without the other, and
#   without nginx.conf and HEALTH_URL above.
#
# There is deliberately no notification setting here. Šank sends nothing
#   outward — no Telegram, no e-mail, no push, no bot. Everything worth the
#   owner's attention is computed and written to the database, and he reads it
#   in the app: the Dnevnik, and the attention list on Puls. If somebody asks
#   you to paste a bot token into this file, the answer is that there is nowhere
#   to put it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AND FOUR THAT MUST NOT BE IN THE SERVER'S FILE.
#
# These are not "optional extras that happen to be unset". Each is a positive
# opt-in whose whole safety comes from being absent on the VPS.
#
# SANK_DEV_ENROL
#   `POST /api/dev/enrol` mints a device token and sets the enrolled-device
#   cookie, with no PIN and no session. It answers 404 unless this is exactly
#   `1`. Setting it on a public server hands an enrolled device — the thing a
#   phone gets after the owner approves it — to anybody who can spell the URL.
#   It is a positive opt-in rather than "off in production" precisely so that no
#   forgotten setting can ever open it. Dev laptops only. (nginx.conf blocks
#   /api/dev/ as well, as a second lock.)
#
# BACKUP_DIR
#   Turns on the in-process hourly backup task, which is skipped entirely when
#   the variable is missing. On the VPS, cron owns backups —
#   /etc/cron.d/sank-backup — because cron keeps taking copies even when the
#   Node process is wedged, and a backup task living inside a wedged process
#   does not. It belongs in a developer's .env, where it gives hourly copies
#   with no cron and no sqlite3 binary to install.
#
# SANK_LEGACY_OPEN
#   Gone. It used to let five Korak 1 routes answer without a session while the
#   waiter screens were frozen; WP8 wired the session into all five and deleted
#   the switch, and tests/unit/tenant.test.ts now asserts there is no way past
#   the authorization check at all. If you find this name in an old .env, delete
#   the line — nothing reads it.
#
# SANK_SCRYPT_LOG2N
#   The work factor for PIN and password hashing: N = 2 ** this, default 14
#   (~50 ms per hash). vitest.config.ts sets it to 10 so a 200-test suite does
#   not spend a minute inside a deliberately slow function. Lowering it on the
#   server makes every stored PIN cheaper to attack, for no gain — the app
#   hashes a PIN when somebody logs in, which is a few times a night. Leave it
#   unset. (Old hashes keep verifying if it ever does change: N is stored inside
#   each hash string.)

# --- Phase 4 -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Where uploaded photos live on the server. NOT under /opt/sank: a release
# directory is replaced on every deploy and the photos must outlive it.
# setup-server.sh creates it owned by `sank`, mode 0700.
#
#   UPLOAD_DIR=/var/lib/sank/uploads
#
# The rclone file list backs up `uploads/delivery/` only. **`uploads/chat/`
# never leaves the VPS** — that is a promise published in *Pravila*, and a
# backup job that quietly copied it would make the page a lie.

# *Prijem sa slike*. Optional: unset means the feature answers
# 503 SCAN_NOT_CONFIGURED and the owner types the delivery in, which is a
# supported state. It belongs in /opt/sank/.env and nowhere else.
#
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
